        'calc.caveat': 'The 1% is set by the Income Tax Law (art. 113-A, third paragraph, section III). The 2.5% comes from the 2026 Revenue Law, which only covers that year. For 2027, the Revenue Law bill has passed the Chamber of Deputies and Senate committees. As of 24 October 2026, it remains in the legislative process. The rate applicable in 2027 will depend on the text published in the Official Gazette.',
        'calc.foot': 'Income Tax Law art. 113-A, third paragraph (2.1% ride-hailing and delivery, 4% lodging, 1% selling through 2025) · 2026 Revenue Law art. 25, section VI, published 07-Nov-2025 (2.5% selling in 2026) · Income Tax Law art. 113-C, section IV (20% without an RFC) · VAT Law art. 18-J, section II, item a) (50% of the VAT; 100% without an RFC), assuming the general 16% VAT rate · Income Tax Law art. 113-B threshold, measured on the preceding tax year · Deadlines: Income Tax Law art. 106 and Federal Tax Code art. 12',
        'calc.pasoFinal': 'Which procedures to do',
        'calc.pasoAdvance': 'Where to file the return',
        'calc.thFinal': 'At what you charge you stay under 300,000 pesos a year. If you also stayed under it last year, you can elect to have the withholding be your final income tax payment; in that case what is withheld is your tax and is not recovered in April.',
        'calc.thAdvance': 'At what you charge you go over 300,000 pesos a year. The final-payment election depends on what you earned the year before, so going over the threshold this year keeps you from using it next year; if you already had it, you lose it and cannot elect it again (Income Tax Law art. 113-B). Without that election, the withholding is an advance you credit in your tax return.',
        'vid.title': 'Withholding and reporting to the SAT, on video',
