        'vid.play': 'Play',
        'vid.pause': 'Pause',
        'vid.replay': 'Replay',
        'vid.textLabel': 'Video transcript',
        'vid.tx1': 'You sell something on a platform and the customer pays in the app. They pay $1,000 for the item plus $160 of VAT, and the platform collects it all. Before paying you, it withholds $25 of income tax and $80 of VAT and pays them to the SAT. It deposits $1,055 to you, before taking its fee. Each month it reports your sales to the SAT and gives you a receipt for what it withheld. What the platform withholds counts toward your taxes as an advance payment, or as your final payment if you elect the Income Tax Law art. 113-B option.',
        'vid.tx2': 'A sale of $1,000 plus $160 of VAT. The platform collects $1,160. If you gave it your RFC, it withholds 2.5% income tax and 50% of the VAT. If you did not, it withholds 20% income tax and all of the VAT. Without an RFC, $255 more is withheld for every $1,000 you sell. The rate depends on whether you gave the platform your RFC, even if you are already registered with the SAT.',
        'vid.tx3': 'Since April 1, 2026, platforms must give the SAT online, real-time access. Access is limited to information in their systems that serves to verify tax compliance. On top of that, each month they report to the SAT on every person who sells or provides services through them. With that data, the SAT can compare what the platform reports with what you file. What the platform records about your transactions reaches the SAT every month, and under article 30-B the SAT can look it up online.',
        'vid.tx4': 'What the platform withholds can be your final payment or an advance. It depends on how much you earned on platforms the year before. If you stayed under 300,000 pesos, you can elect to make the withholding your final payment. If you went over, the withholding is an advance and you file every month. If you go over the threshold in a year, the option closes from the next year on. With the final payment you do not file monthly returns for that income, but what is withheld is not recovered. If you had the option and lose it, you cannot elect it again.',
        'map.titleA': 'Lodging tax in Mexico\'s 32 federal entities,',
        'map.titleMark': 'from 2% to 6%',
        'map.lead': 'The lodging tax is set by each state, not by the federal government, and each state congress picks its own rate. In 27 of Mexico\'s 32 federal entities (31 states and Mexico City) the platform is required to withhold or remit it, so you are probably already paying it. In several states the rate for apartments and houses is higher than the hotel rate. Tap your state.',
